{"clock":2,"ops":[{"kind":"set-notes","author":"ee004d31386b76440a74651c79871f156633d4ff","time":1793007067,"data":{"notes":"# v0.11.0: git-board keeps its own issues, and git-bug is gone\n\ngit-board no longer runs on git-bug. Issues, comments and people are git-board's own, stored in git under `refs/board/`. They travel with `git push` and `git pull`, work offline and need no database. git-board syncs them with GitHub and GitLab itself, and is MIT licensed.\n\n## Before you upgrade: switch each repository once\n\nA repository whose issues are still git-bug's (`refs/bugs`) shows an empty board, with a banner saying so, until it is switched.\n\n1. Stop git-bug's bridge sync wherever it runs (CI, cron, other clones).\n2. `git board switch` reads git-bug's issues and pulls them from the forges. It reports what it brings, what it can't, and what the next sync would do. Nothing is written.\n3. `git board switch --apply` writes the issues into `refs/board/` and marks the repository as switched. It never deletes `refs/bugs`, and never pushes.\n4. `git board sync --check`, then `git board sync`. This sends the issues and the switch mark to `origin`.\n5. Every other clone runs `git board sync`. That brings the switch along; nobody else switches.\n\nOnce no clone or automation uses git-bug any more, its refs can be removed (`refs/bugs`, `refs/identities` and `.git/git-bug`).\n\n## What's new\n\n**Issues in git, synced with the forges**\n- A new core library keeps issues, comments and people as logs of operations in git, with a local search index.\n- Two-way sync with GitHub and GitLab: issues, comments, labels, assignees and states. Later edits win, and a tie goes to the forge.\n- Private content never reaches a public project unless you allow it (`publish-private`). Confidential issues are never synced.\n- Each forge account's token comes from git's credential helper. Environment tokens are only used for their own host. The forges are reached over https only.\n\n**One sync**\n- `git board sync` runs everything in order: git pull, the forges, the GitHub Project's fields, then git push. `--check` shows what it would do and writes nothing; `--git` syncs with git alone.\n- Settings › Sync: one table with `origin`, each forge and the GitHub Project, plus Check and Sync. It replaces the separate Sync page.\n- Forges are found from `origin`'s URLs, so most repositories need no `[forges]` table.\n- No issue is created on a forge without your consent, either the first time a repository syncs with that forge or when a run would create 50 or more. Use `--allow-create`, or tick \"I understand\" on the page.\n- A forge that can't be read is left out of the run with a warning, and the others still sync. Only a failed git pull stops the push.\n- Project numbers (GB-12, CO2-3075) are carried over by the switch, and the sync sends them to `origin` itself.\n\n**The web app**\n- `#/issues/GB-36` and `#/issues/#36` open the issue directly.\n- Labels are read and written through git-board's own GitHub code, not the `gh` CLI.\n- The Releases page shows this clone's releases at once, then checks `origin` in the background.\n- `git board switch` shows its progress as it goes.\n- The Gitea option is gone, and so is the git-bug wording.\n\n**For developers**\n- `make help` lists the targets. `make install` now builds the web app first.\n- `make docs` builds the documentation site (guide, spec, decisions) from `docs/`; `make docs-serve` previews it.\n- Tests can't reach the network or your tokens (`internal/hermetic`).\n- The licence check now also covers the web app's npm packages.\n\n## Breaking changes\n- git-board doesn't read git-bug's data any more: switch each repository first (see above).\n- `git board sync` now syncs the forges too, so it may write to GitHub and GitLab. `git board sync --git` gives you the old git-only sync.\n- The `#/sync` page moved to Settings › Sync.\n- Gitea is no longer supported.\n- With no `[forges]` table, the forges are now found from `origin`'s URLs. A table still overrides that.\n\n**Full changes:** #55 (the rewrite), #56 (one sync, forge discovery, fixes), #57 (release prep).\n"}}]}